    "case_summary": "An 82-year-old man was hospitalized with several episodes of dizziness, syncope, and shortness of breath that began one day before admission. Medical history included coronary artery disease with stent placement 10 years ago, and surgically treated colon cancer.\n\nVital signs on admission:- Heart rate: 114 beats/min- Blood pressure: 94/62 mm Hg- Oxygen saturation: 91% on 4 L/min of supplemental oxygen- Respiratory rate: 28 breaths per minuteInitial 12-lead electrocardiogram showed no evidence of acute myocardial ischemia or arrhythmias.\n\n- Laboratory findings:  * Troponin I level: 0.135 µg/L (peaked at 0.499 µg/L)  * D-dimer level: >109.52 nmol/L  * N-terminal pro brain-type natriuretic peptide level: 562.21 pmol/L- Imaging Studies:  * Transthoracic echocardiogram: Dilated right ventricle with reduced systolic function, flattened interventricular septum, pulmonary hypertension, and small hyperdynamic left ventricle    * CT Angiogram (Initial):    Title: Initial Chest CT Angiogram    Description: Acute extensive partially occlusive pulmonary emboli in distal main pulmonary arteries extending into lobar and segmental branches of upper and lower lobes bilaterally. Dilated right ventricle with RV-LV ratio >1.5    * Follow-up CT:    Title: Post-procedure Chest CT    Description: Alveolar filling process with interlobular septal thickening, mixed ground-glass and centrilobular opacities in right upper and middle lobes",
